From 55ce7dd8d16fdd46eb2fdb452bf0ecba7d817d32 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(inventory): present authored native containers

Bind authored container geometry to authoritative Java menus, slots and cursor state without changing inventory request ownership.

CPU geometry, original-machine probes and meaningful widget interaction
tests establish these bounded behaviors; native GPU/font-family parity,
live container transfers and complete custom responses remain separate gates.

Share bounded native scene, layout, image and text primitives between modal
forms and real container presentation. Negotiate a separate container
program, bound to the current connection, actual ChestMenu object, menu ID
and increasing sequence. Retain vanilla stacks, carried items, state
revisions, click/release/quickcraft dispatch and same-menu close semantics.
Use the same clipped native slot rectangle for rendering and pointer hits.

Reproduce pinned native progress and transient slot-to-slot flight consumers
through authored flags and real menu state changes. Keep typed count labels
at their authored bounds with Java count formatting once; vanilla retains
carried decorations. Read exact retained native lock bytes and the owned
connection's showTags rule. Unknown required storage/filter presentation or
unsupported visible renderers keeps the complete stock container screen.
No cursor collection, item snapshot, native request address or false
inactive-item binding is manufactured for presentation.

Reject visible inventory buttons with unsupported action mappings before
enabling native presentation; retain the stock screen instead of inert
controls. Split tooltip components on actual newlines even without an
authored width. Normalize CRLF across styled runs and preserve empty lines,
colors, emphasis and bounded wrapping without rewriting literal escapes.

Original target index and fresh scheduler fixtures cover explicit, hidden
and percentage sizes. Java layout regressions cover positions, anchors,
offsets, constraints, malformed coordinates and unchanged template cells.
Visit only actual bounded children; retain strict container completeness
until intentional authored subsets receive a separate admission review.
Native pixels, actor delivery and live Social presentation remain open.

Original 1.26.51.1/build 51061372 vector updater execution establishes
coefficient interpolation, progress clamps, the duration floor and signed
offsets. All 1,536 mixed-unit native samples across 32 easing selectors
match the add-on within 1.5e-4 after layout resolution. Targeted tests cover
viewport changes, hit bounds, reset/play and dependent dimensions. Native
GPU comparison, container animation lifecycle and the other output types
remain open requirements; these CPU checks do not establish GUI parity.

Share the native animation manager with authored container scenes. Dispatch
entrance-push and scrollbar events, relayout animated size/offset controls,
and retain clocks across authoritative binding refreshes by hierarchical
control and inventory identity. Newly inserted controls start at the update
time; removed and destroyed controls retire without losing slot addresses.

Pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 ui_common.json defines the container entrance
offset and player-portrait size tracks. Zero-size entrance frames retain
their authoritative slot mapping while rejecting pointer input. Inventory
item and flight opacity is carried with each extracted GUI item and applied
at the final atlas or oversized texture blit with premultiplied tint, so
cached textures and ordinary Java item users remain independent.

Targeted lifecycle, container refresh, animated picking, scrolling, and
premultiplied vertex tests pass. Exit/pop transitions, carried-item opacity,
inactive filters, broader workstation coverage, and native/direct/proxy
visual parity remain required; this does not establish full GUI coverage.

Resolve color animation references and run four-channel image-component
tracks through the shared forms, HUD and container renderer. Keep easing
overshoot until final color submission, multiply image color alpha by
control opacity once, and retain color clocks across binding refreshes.

Accept version-3 storage scenes for hopper, dispenser, dropper, barrel,
ender chest and shulker box menus. Match the real Java menu family and
slot count before claiming ownership, then reuse the native scene and
actual-slot geometry with ordinary inventory packet authority. Retain
the original screen object only for failure recovery; never synthesize
a chest screen for another menu type. Preserve count-one activation,
contents ordering, replacement lifetime and stale-menu rejection.

Register the separate bidirectional native furnace preference channel and
retain tab, filter and layout choices per family across screen openings.
Discard queued updates and disconnect callbacks from an older connection,
and clear preferences when their owner disconnects. Keep unknown native
enum values intact, and send edits only when the server advertises the
channel. This prepares native furnace controllers; the cooking scenes and
their native/direct/ViaProxy comparisons remain incomplete.

Bind container payloads, pending contents, action acknowledgments and
click authority to the current ClientPacketListener. Java reconfiguration
can reuse a physical connection, menu number and sequence. Begin a new
play session explicitly and reject retired receivers and disconnects
before they consume its pending presentation or send inventory actions.
Tests cover reconfiguration on the same connected transport, reused menu
identity, delayed contents and acknowledgments, and session cleanup.

Present the bundled brewing-stand program over the actual five-slot menu.
Keep normal Java inventory operations and receive signed native progress
counters separately from Java short properties. Bind every update to the
play listener, menu object, kind and presentation sequence; retired updates
cannot change a newly opened workstation. Resolve singleton fuel and input
cells alongside the three bottle cells and all player inventory slots.

Receive authoritative cooking snapshots and raw progress in the production
client. Bind retained state to the exact play listener, menu object, family
and monotonic opening/update identities. Reject stale frames before item
conversion, retire old authority on a malformed newer frame, and retain
the sequence floor when a menu closes so reused IDs cannot revive it.

Share ordinary container and cooking presentation frames over the real
menu renderer. Copy translated recipe previews, keep their references
separate from inventory slots, and retain raw progress without changing
item authority. Tests resolve all 39 inventory slots through real layout
and cover menu reuse, reconnects, invalid conversion and progress updates.

Keep ordinary cooking controls for scenes with required input that is not
yet implemented. Complete cursor semantics, search/navigation and the
remaining authored handlers before replacing the default cooking screen.

Match authored close commands to their source input and pressed mapping.
Cooking slot buttons also map menu_cancel to try_menu_exit; that mapping
must not close a menu on an ordinary primary or secondary pointer click.
Use one clipped, front-to-back control order for close buttons and slots,
including equal-layer overlays, disabled controls and transparent frames.
A headless screen regression covers both pointer buttons on 63 real menu
slots with the cooking cancel mapping and fails with the original handler.
Native cooking activation and remaining controls are still incomplete.

Negotiate typed cooking action and result payloads in the Fabric play
session. Retain the original cell and snapshot through follow-up phases,
including synchronous bridge replies and newer predicted frames. Admit
initial input only after resolving its authored scope. Match replies to
the complete action, serialize independent gestures, and require a newer
frame after rejected or consumed authority. Retire pending input after
capability withdrawal, menu replacement, transport ambiguity or timeout.
Keep request floors across same-listener menu reuse.

Default cooking programs still use ordinary controls. This client transport
does not establish native device equivalence, cross-input prediction,
completed default controls or native visual parity. Regression tests exercise the
state transitions without a GPU or a live server.

Route admitted authored recipe cells through the production sender and
bind action authority to the installed native screen. Carry the resolved
recipe scope into clipped front-to-back geometry. Consume captured button
presses, drags, releases and duplicate releases independently of vanilla
inventory dispatch; retain the original cell when newer frames replace it.
Declare Java primary/secondary pointer presses as phase zero and releases
as phase two. Native physical device equivalence remains unverified.

Retain a one-shot terminal release after retirement or ambiguous transport
so a failed presentation cannot strand a primary gesture. Release only on
the same connected listener and actual menu, preserve increasing request
identity, and never retry an ambiguous terminal send. Restore the exact
original screen even after controller ownership retires, without replacing
another screen or a reused menu. Keep unsupported required controls on the ordinary menu rather than
installing an inert authored view. The default search/navigation/help controls, focus,
tertiary/controller bindings and complete native presentation stay open.

Headless integration tests execute resolved authored cells, the real sender,
frame replacement, same-menu restoration, capability withdrawal and
ambiguous-send cleanup. Disabling pointer routing fails the new interaction
regression. These controls do not prove live primary transfer acceptance,
GPU output or native physical input parity.

Reject unsupported recipe mapping attributes and require actual boolean
flags. The pointer adapter cannot honor select/deselect constraints and
must not turn those mappings into an unconditional recipe transfer. Both
the missing-attribute guard and string-boolean acceptance are covered by
the authored geometry regression.

Route supported navigation and filtering toggles through the existing
per-family preference transport. Resolve their real authored group/index
and checked state, keep clipped painter order with slots and recipes,
and wait for a newer matching cooking snapshot before accepting more
input. Do not interrupt pending recipe gestures. Capability loss, timeout
and ambiguous preference sends restore the exact original menu screen.

The unchanged target callbacks146c99520 and146c99b40 pass 74 controls
over supplied controller/model state, with downstream tab/filter setters,
collection rebuild and settings refresh observed at their call boundary.
Their event index and checked fields remain distinct. Pointer handlers
cover current selection, filtered state, pending snapshots and failure
recovery. Layout, focus, search and controller mappings remain unfinished;
unsupported required controls continue to use ordinary screens.

Connect direct layout toggles through the same displayed-snapshot
preference route. Preserve current tab and filtering, validate the native
layout indices, skip an already-selected layout and wait for a matching
new model after either direction. Read the cached forced-profile state
before admission; unsupported forced-layout controls restore the original
screen without accepting input.

The unchanged native callback146c99560 passes 480 controls over supplied
layout, tab, remember flag, input mode and source-validity boundaries.
It refreshes only on a changed layout and has additional saved-tab and
held-selection branches. Those internal states and their producers remain
unimplemented in this preference adapter; full controller, device and
visual parity are not established by this route.

Carry the native controller mode explicitly in version-five cooking
inputs and retain it on follow-up phases and terminal cancellation. Permit
an initial mode-two primary focus before a primary selection, including
when the focus acknowledgment is still pending. Other initial contexts
retain their prior phase admission. The Java mouse adapter supplies mode
one; this change does not reinterpret a pointer as a native touch device.

Preserve each focus/selection's own authority, pending-action identity and
release context across newer frames, capability withdrawal and ambiguous
sends. An older focus rejection cannot erase a newer active selection and
strand its terminal release. Reject unrelated commands during the verified
focus path. End focus cancellation at most once without another transfer.

The core compares the focused carried-item requests with fresh original
native graph, selection, transfer and serialization execution. Client
controls exercise reentrant focus replies, captured context, cancellation,
forged acknowledgments and late focus failure. Physical mode-two event
production, carried-item screen controls and complete native UI parity
remain unfinished; the normal mouse route keeps its existing guard.

Retain supported cooking scenes while their real menu cursor changes.
Ordinary pickup and put-back keep the same Java menu and slot dispatch;
vanilla carried-item extraction retains its existing ownership. Do not
retire the presentation merely because a stack is carried. The mouse
recipe sender still rejects a direct press with a nonempty cursor.

The complete pinned tab and filter callbacks146c99520 and146c99b40 do
not read the held item. Admit those preferences with a carried stack,
while retaining the idle-gesture and newer-matching-model checks. Keep
layout changes guarded because callback146c99560 also updates saved tab
and held selection, whose producers remain unfinished. This does not
establish native carried opacity, focused-device input or default UI parity.

Retain native Input/Toggle state and release captures across bound container
scene refreshes. Queue component changes before applying button scope and
consume emitted checked values instead of inverting controller snapshots.
Compose non-consuming preference events before publishing their final options,
so simultaneous tab and filter changes do not overwrite each other. Keep
local collection/cell selection independent of directional keyboard focus.

Original Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build51061372 callback registration, receiver and
ScreenView delivery cover632 cases. Another96 composed native factory scenes
cover384 button steps across modes, scope, radio state and capture. The main
container consumer and preference adapter match those steps. Preserve the
explicit admission limits for global button reinjection, unsupported screen
input policies and unresolved toggle collection indices. Default cooking
search/slider handlers, full controller construction and device/pixel parity
remain separate requirements.

Reject unsupported screen-owned routes and focused slot actions before
input can reach them. Full-scope menus retain ordinary controls rather than
exposing a focused action without its native request handler.

Use the scroll viewport for pointer clipping so retained offscreen controls
remain eligible for directional selection without receiving hidden clicks.
A regression fails with the owner rectangle and passes with the viewport.

Admit implicit recipe-cell hover only for cooking presentations that own
its controller path. Consume both hover phases and clear the selection
only while its collection and signed cell index still match. This prevents
an older exit from erasing a newer selection across binding refreshes.
Read collection indices with native integral type and narrowing rules.
Original callback registration, dispatcher and final exit clearing cover
5,832 cases, with collection resolution and first selection/exit virtual
methods supplied as observed boundaries. Full default screens, touch-held
state and storage-item selection predicates remain separate requirements.

Consume native tab increment/decrement activation events through the shared
CookingNavigation category model. Preserve furnace/filter/layout options,
clear selected cells on a tab refresh and wait for matching authoritative
preferences before accepting another gesture. Support dedicated authored
buttons and navigation input panels, including zero-size global panels.
Require preference transport before enabling those controls. Keep held-item
panel commands, mixed non-consuming inventory routes and screen-host input
preservation explicit remaining integration work.
Original registration, callback dispatch and category-query execution cover
8,064 cases with selected-tab setter and model refresh observed separately.
Pointer and keyboard screen regressions cover phase retention, stale model
responses, carried items, selection clearing and missing capability fallback.

Retain cooking screen hosts as real scene controls and admit supported
host-owned navigation through the same retained input component state as
buttons and input panels. Preserve the root across authoritative updates
so a held activation does not retrigger. Use explicit authored screen graphs
in interaction fixtures. Require the versioned core contract for an
active player-hurt close policy before enabling the screen.
The host-navigation regression fails against the prior content-only core
and exercises press, update-while-held, release and subsequent activation.
Full default host event routing, held-input/reset, search/edit controls,
devices and native pixels remain required work.

Route authored VIEW arrow activations through retained input components
instead of navigating directly on Java key callbacks. Repeat and release
phases therefore follow the native mapping and input mode. Focus-enabled
panels participate even without a controller action. Reveal and relayout
the selected control before updating the logical pointer, then deliver
nested hover buttons before the outer controller queue. Retire arrow
phases while preference input is unavailable and across model refreshes.

Original 1.26.51.1/protocol2193 ScreenView execution matches 147 composed
cases, 2,169 focus/pointer/hover steps and 840 controller button events.
The capture supplies input order and ScreenView fields, uses original
component factories and the base controller, and retains kind-5 pointer
events as an unsupported controller-delivery boundary. Full native screen
construction, focus-tab groups, modal/deflection routing, scroll geometry,
physical devices and pixels remain unfinished.

Reinject GLOBAL targets through raw component input using the event's
previous phase, then forward the original controller event. Retain
physical pointer coordinates separately from focus hover coordinates.
Implicit GLOBAL hover uses the current View input mode and event time,
not its zero-mode header. Native phase two has no binary controller
callback and must not become a hover exit. Recursive GLOBAL wildcards
remain rejected before input.

Original raw mapping, GLOBAL recursion, capture priority, focus and hover
execute in 29 View cases and 200 steps. Another 240 composed Toggle and
Furnace callback steps match component state and domain effects; immediate
settings-notification timing remains outside that adapter. Original
component, manager, controller and capture-map node/vector cleanup finish
with no native allocations. View fields/order, bare controls, empty child
controllers and downstream preference setters remain supplied boundaries.
Full controller pointer delivery, focus-tab groups, modal/deflection,
physical input and pixels remain unfinished.

Retain ordinary cooking screens when GLOBAL targets or cross-control
aliases would authorize inventory, recipe or close actions that still use
a separate direct pointer controller. This capability boundary preserves
action ownership until those receivers use the native event path. Tests
cover preference admission, alias rejection and fallback before actions.

Route authored VIEW tab activations through visible, enabled navigation_tab
controls in native tree order. Choose the nearest authored index, retain
last-checked and duplicate-index ownership, update the eligible group and
emit its non-notifying change before old/new control-local hover events.
Keep logical and physical View pointers unchanged. Admit keyboard mappings
by their VIEW receiver instead of applying controller-command validation.

Original 1.26.51.1/protocol2193 traversal, tab mutation and Furnace callback
execution matches 208 cases and 2,117 steps, including 815 group changes
and 906 controller buttons. All 79,461 native frees finish with no native
allocations. Screen update, downstream preference setters and settings
notification timing remain observed boundaries. Default Furnace tabs explicitly disable focus. The active focus-enabled
VIEW lifecycle is integrated below. Full native screen
construction, controller pointer delivery, devices and pixels remain open.

Use forced native focus selection for focus-enabled VIEW tabs, reveal and
relayout their target, then deliver whole-view hover before the group
change. Retain both stored View pointer positions. Keep non-focus tabs on
their separate group-change-first, control-local hover route.

Do not manufacture the native selection-latch flag from FocusManager
ownership. Original overlapping/consuming controls show that focus and
hover can have different owners; the extra flag incorrectly activates a
second focused mapping and changes the controller preference twice.

Original 1.26.51.1/protocol2193 execution matches 204 composed cases and
2,388 steps, with 804 group changes and 1,182 controller button events.
All 127,116 native frees finish with no native allocations. Fixtures
supply active manager state, tree/order, geometry and initial bindings.
Full screen updates and downstream setters remain observation boundaries.
Manager activation and inactive delivery, native scroll reveal, edit-box
selection, Button audio, controller pointer receivers, full
screen construction, physical devices and pixels remain unfinished.

Apply the actual Button appearance component and its consume_event result
to retained container input. Preserve press state separately from applied
visibility, including suppressed mapping transitions, phase two and
matching scene replacements. Apply named visibility before layout, then
refresh geometry and draw order without creating another component event.

Keep own visibility/enabled resets distinct from inherited enabled-event
updates. Relay implicit hover buttons directly to the controller queue;
they do not re-enter the sibling Button component. Let raw component
callbacks run before later routed controls read their context.

Original component, property, hover and ScreenView captures cover 1,268
cases and 16,900 steps, with 185,262 native frees and balanced owners.
ScreenView raw input returns void; compare its deliveries and retained
state, while measuring consumption at the original Input component.
Full screen construction/update timing, Button audio, pointer receivers,
manager activation, physical devices and pixels remain separate gates.

Apply native visible/ignored callbacks to retained Input mappings before
layout and use inherited visibility to remove hidden controls from normal
input order. The complete original ScreenView update clears hidden hover,
invalidates mappings on removal, and preserves captures and double-press
history. A captured hidden GLOBAL release still reaches its component;
showing only an ancestor does not erase that release phase. Do not synthesize
implicit-hover controller buttons from the hidden hover callback.

Compare 144 original 1.26.51.1 full pending-update fixtures across modes,
visible/ignored owner and parent changes, mappings and capture policies.
The 2,592 steps and 1,452 controller buttons match with native allocation
and owner cleanup balanced. Twelve initially hidden native fixtures prove
phase invalidation during initial registration. External notification queue
insertion, the admitted modal root, geometry and clocks are supplied. Full
binding/frame scheduling, modal transitions, devices and pixels remain open.

Create and index every retained input component before resolving Button
names. Native named setters immediately notify the target Input before its
Button callback, including nested and aliased controls. Keep inherited
visibility, hover exit and normal input registration in the later View
update so subsequent controls in the same raw pass retain native delivery.
Restore matching Java scene copies without manufacturing property events.

Use the host visibility writer for runtime setters and preserve reentrant
sender updates. The original tree constructor and by-value notification
methods now populate and deduplicate the real pending queues. Complete
native update/drain and ownership cleanup cover 216 composed, self-control
and wildcard fixtures, with no queue/state output supplied by a stub.
Root attachment, trees/geometry, mode and drain boundaries remain supplied.
Binding-expression execution, complete screen construction, physical frame
scheduling, Button audio and devices remain open.

Restore cached Button visuals onto matching scene copies without replaying
intermediate aliased visibility callbacks or retiring held child mappings.

Initialize supported cooking Input mappings once after the first accepted
scene is prepared. The complete original View wrapper starts its mappings
at zero, then the initial mode notification queues the root update that
invalidates them to two before activation and controller delivery.
Repeated Java refreshes and resize preparation must preserve held input.

The original complete wrapper constructor/destructor, resize, mode entry
and activation/frame paths cover 144 initial-input fixtures and 864 raw
steps with 34,020 balanced frees. The actual screen regression fails on
the published implementation before this correction. Bare control trees,
empty external layout work queues and client/CRT services remain supplied;
outer installation, physical notifications, derived cooking controllers
and nonempty authored layout/binding construction remain separate gates.

Admit the current desktop pointer after Gui.setScreen resynchronizes it
and before Minecraft renders the first frame. Without that notification,
stationary hover selection waits for physical movement. Convert Java HUD
coordinates through the existing native viewport and require the same
screen to remain installed. Refresh and resize do not replay admission.

The whole original installer and optional wrapper/View pointer path match
96 cases, 480 raw steps and 282 controller buttons against the published
component implementation, with 25,602 balanced native frees. The complete
original client mode callback and its native screen lambda execute another
72 sequences and 216 notifications, with 18,480 balanced frees. Client
services, foreground enumeration, empty layout work and bare trees remain
supplied; physical mode admission and derived cooking controllers stay
separate gates. The screen regression covers post-resync pointer data,
unequal Java/native scales, selection, matching refreshes and stale screens.

Request negotiated player inventory openings at the actual Gui admission
attempt. Constructors alone never send an open. Share the route between
direct Bedrock and ViaProxy, retain one opening through presentation
swaps, and give ordinary reopens a new token despite Java's persistent
menu zero. Accept close notifications only for the matching connection
and opening. Keep legacy direct opening at the installed-screen boundary.

Use shared native count text for authored slot labels, retaining raw numeric
binding quantities and genuine Java quickcraft preview overrides. Bedrock
1.26.60.5 (protocol 2225) hides counts below two and caps values above 99 at
99+. The add-on previously printed the full decimal count, which diverged
from native labels for larger custom stacks.

All 536 controls execute the complete original formatter from executable
SHA-256 988f6a61403b4381aa41e6c66d64cce441b14e561a9b10f647c2758ef3f2672a,
with caller storage and CRT strlen/memcpy supplied. This validates count text,
not quickcraft semantics, UI timing or pixels. Full player/controller
publication remains unfinished.

Retain typed player/workbench snapshots independently of the ordinary
screen. Bind commands to the exact connection, menu object, opening,
container generation, revision and request. Buffer search edits while
a command awaits its reply, including the pending-to-acknowledged
generation transition, and ignore stale results or hover exits.

Share recipe-action maps across player and cooking presentations. Keep
the real 46-slot player/workbench menu and full component previews rather
than constructing a second inventory authority. Retire a rejected
presentation for the whole opening instead of retrying each update.

Resolve the real How to Play and Encyclopedia programs as local views of
an opening. Preserve the underlying inventory input, animation lifecycle,
search, recipe sources and cursor across the guide and its Back/Escape
paths. Deliver releases to the suspended inventory input so a Help click
cannot leave the previous View held. Keep native topic toggles and expand
instructions through this client's actual key bindings without discarding
native text styles.

The pinned 1.26.60.5 Help callback selects Survival topic5 and Creative
Encyclopedia topic47. A physical Survival capture confirms Your First
Craft. Both graphs travel with the player snapshot. Visible unsupported
inline image glyphs retain ordinary controls instead of exposing tokens.
Full installed Help lifetimes and input-glyph appearance remain unverified.

Route authored real-slot confirmation and crafting-output mappings through
the existing Java menu/backend boundary, including keyboard releases,
cursor drop and search focus. Admit only the inactive native bundle cursor
wrapper; actual bundle contents still require their renderer. Real default
player/workbench scenes retain all46 slots and original recipe identities.

Recipe auxiliary actions and native held-selection commands restore the
ordinary menu before issuing their unimplemented action. Bulk recipes,
Creative cursor replacement, native held-selection state, complete input
routing and installed pixels remain unfinished. No unsupported action is
silently implemented as a no-op.

Carry item lighting and opacity together from extraction through ordinary
atlas and oversized-item submission. Each extracted item owns immutable
style while the model texture remains shared. Restore both values after
nested extraction or failure. The native inactive-item filter uses the
shared typed light multiplier and RGB conversion without changing alpha;
control opacity still modulates the premultiplied submitted texture.

Remove the inactive-renderer admission rejection. The real default screen
regression now contains an unavailable recipe, and atlas submission tests
check native quarter lighting, typed overrides, opacity composition and
independent opaque users. Complete native item geometry and pixel parity
still require installed comparison.

Read storage bars from the versioned native Item snapshot, independently
of Java bundle contents and their fractional capacity rules. Preserve the
native integer weight as float bindings in the correct slot/cursor scope.
Missing, unknown-version or incomplete snapshots keep ordinary controls;
a known empty bundle can retain its actual closed item preview without
rejecting the whole Creative catalog.

The actual default-screen fixture retains an empty bundle and an inactive
recipe. A separate consumer boundary holds native weight 22 over an empty
Java bundle component and checks the selected binding scope. This does
not establish native item pixels or interactive bundle-tooltip parity.
Until the tooltip collection, placement and selection renderer is ported,
hovering or carrying storage items explicitly restores ordinary controls
before an incomplete native tooltip can silently consume its input.

Resolve fixed recipe grids through bounded cell windows over the complete
retained catalog. Converge the window after measuring the authored scroll
viewport and retain neighboring rows around logical keyboard focus. Resize
and scroll changes keep original collection indices, action identities,
menu state, and the full logical scroll extent. Local window changes do
not advance the authoritative snapshot revision.

Copy only instantiated previews into each view frame while retaining the
complete preview cache. Rebuild windows after keyboard delivery so focus
callbacks do not replace component state during nested event dispatch.
Passive source-less hover bindings are not recipe action transitions and
must not retire the screen during directional navigation. Actual
unsupported recipe commands continue to restore ordinary controls.

The fixed 18 by 18 cell contract follows crafting.scroll_grid and its
recipe-book template in the pinned 1.26.60.5 inventory_screen.json. This
preserves authored geometry without claiming identical native pooling.

The pinned Creative layout binds hotbar auto-place to auto-destroy. Keep
that declared command in the explicit unsupported-host-action path so a
closed catalog can render without authorizing destruction. Both mapped
host commands and Java quick-move on those authored hotbar slots restore
ordinary controls before changing items or sending a request. Test both
recipe-book and Creative layouts, including the refused destruction path.

Retain input ancestor geometry while the immutable layout root is unchanged.
Invalidate it when the scene is prepared again, and continue updating the
current draw visibility and viewport on every input-layout call. Collection
windows likewise need rescanning only after layout or keyboard focus changes.
Render the shared presentation's stable layer order directly instead of
allocating and sorting a second list for each frame.

A Survival inventory JFR profile identifies collection metadata and repeated
input traversal among the render-thread costs. Existing pointer, focus,
scrolling, relayout and screen lifecycle regressions validate these cache
boundaries; hardware performance and native visual parity need live checks.

Reuse parsed container hit regions while draw boxes, painter order,
enablement and the positive-opacity boundary remain unchanged. Box
identity covers immutable layout bounds, clips and scene action mappings.
Scene refreshes and relayouts create new boxes and rebuild validation;
hover branches and zero-opacity transitions invalidate the cached regions.
This removes per-frame action-map parsing from stable inventories without
retaining input targets after they become hidden, disabled or moved.
A regression covers active fades, hidden and disabled slots, restored
controls, layout movement and removal from the draw list.

Start a Java GUI stratum at each cumulative authored layer boundary.
The shared presentation already orders these layers, so the renderer need
not search earlier layers again to recover overlap order for every quad.
Equal-layer controls retain authored order and ordinary overlap handling.
This bounds Java's GUI ordering work without changing native hit regions
or inventory packet ownership.

A controlled replacement of this method in the same isolated Survival
session reduces render-thread overlap-search samples. Static recipe,
slot-grid and crafting-background pixels remain identical before and
after the replacement. Software-renderer timing is diagnostic evidence,
not a performance guarantee for player hardware or native pixel parity.

Suppress gameplay HUD extraction while an authored native container is the
current screen. Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5 (protocol 2225), with a log in
the main hand and shield in the offhand, keeps both held items behind the
Survival inventory but hides the gameplay hotbar, health and hunger.
The installed add-on control incorrectly draws those HUD elements there.

Gate the HUD call by the current native-container screen instead of
changing the local HUD setting or server visibility state. Closing or
replacing the screen restores normal HUD extraction immediately. Keep
world and first-person item rendering independent of this screen rule.
This does not establish shield-pose or complete inventory pixel parity.

Retain container painter traversal while the layout, pointer, input
visual state and sampled opacity stay unchanged. The installed Survival
inventory profile spends time in repeated property-map lookups during
NativeUiPresentation.collect, even after static animation-manager walks
were removed. Continue rendering items, portraits, image color and texture
animations every frame; only reuse the accepted draw list and input frames.

Toggle changes advance the input visual revision, including keyboard tab
selection, so unchanged pointer coordinates cannot retain an old checked
branch. Alpha sampling supplies a separate revision for fades and reset
events. Replaced layout roots invalidate geometry and presentation together.
Targeted controls preserve fades and a same-pointer toggle transition.
This does not establish full modpack frame-time or native GPU parity.

Offer a persistent Bedrock inventory screens setting for clients that need
ordinary Java controls. Gate storage, cooking and player presentation before
scene construction and recheck delayed installation. Keep the default
enabled for server resource packs that depend on authored layouts. This
changes presentation only; core retains inventory and request authority.

Run authored screen.exit_pop tracks before removing a container, then
close only on screen.exit_end while the same screen and menu still own
the client. Repeated close requests do not restart the animation. Scenes
without an authored completion path keep immediate closing.

Consume pointer, keyboard, drag and slot mutations during the outgoing
scene. Stop a held recipe request and deactivate native actions when the
exit starts, while retaining presentation resources until removal. Show
the gameplay HUD behind that scene, as in the target Bedrock 1.26.60.5
Escape controls. The default authored container tracks provide the exit
duration; no host timeout substitutes for their completion event.

Tests cover deferred closure, input retirement, one-time completion,
replacement screens and retired menu ownership.

Apply close_on_player_hurt through the shared player-owned pending state,
original server damage cause and 100-update gate. Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5
runtime controls establish that a false scene policy still starts the gate
and that gated events are consumed rather than deferred. Use the existing
pop-exit lifecycle instead of sampling health or closing on every hurt.
Admit that policy only when the program advertises the core hurt contract.
Legacy cores retain ordinary controls instead of omitting the policy.
Keep the separate controller and visible-control gates for unfinished
cooking bindings. Client-predicted damage and exact frame timing remain
unverified.

Storage presentation now receives the complete native host graph,
including background and animation controls. Test fixtures declare their
screen content controls explicitly. The cooking boundary test covers
both negotiated policy admission and ordinary fallback without the core
contract, before any inventory input is dispatched.

Exercise physical category and layout selection through exported player-inventory programs in both recipe-book and Creative layouts. The regression rejects index-zero preference commands when the authored controls require Construction or a different layout, retaining unrelated tabs and crafting preferences.

Map Shift-left and Shift-right catalog clicks to menu_auto_place for the
retained player inventory. The versioned 1.26.60 graph maps that source to
recipe_tertiary; official local-world controls confirm full-stack auto-place
with an unchanged cursor. Retain the pressed source through mouse release
so releasing Shift first cannot leave the authored input pressed. Physical
regressions cover both buttons, repeated clicks, modifier release and the
following ordinary click through the exported player program.

Bind the Creative catalog-only toolbar's authored container_auto_destroy
command to an optional backend channel before queuing a command. Preserve
ordinary transfers in other layouts and restore the ordinary screen when
the backend lacks this capability. Shift-left and Shift-right use the
native menu_auto_place mapping and retain the original source on release.

Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 local-world controls discard a full
hotbar stack with either chord, preserve an occupied cursor, and leave an
empty slot unchanged. The inventory-only layout instead moves the stack
into main inventory. Matching inventory_screen.json declarations provide
the toolbar override. These controls establish visible behavior, not
original-client request-byte equality.

Dispatch authored quick-move commands directly for player slot chords.
Java's pointer gesture falls back to pickup when the cursor is occupied,
which swaps the held stack with the hotbar stack in the installed native
inventory layout. Bedrock 1.26.60.5 preserves the cursor and moves the
selected stack into main inventory. Retain the real menu's quick-move
transport while bypassing only Java's incompatible pointer classification.
Occupied-cursor regressions cover inventory-only and recipe-book layouts.

Clear the parent GUI focus owner when the player search editor loses
focus on an outside click, disappearance or screen exit. Clearing only the
editor flag leaves it registered as the focused child. Java26.3 skips
reselecting that same child, so a later click cannot restore text input
and the inventory key can close the screen while typing a new query.

Installed direct controls exposed this after a recipe click. A real
EditBox screen regression reproduces lost text input before the fix, then
accepts another query containing the inventory key after refocusing.
This fixes Java focus bookkeeping; it does not change native search or
inventory action semantics.

Retain the latest local search edit at connection scope before serializing
its opening-bound command. Restore only that text into a fresh presentation,
which sends a new search under its own opening, generation and revision.
Closing a menu may discard pending commands and queued transport work; it
must not discard unsent edits or an explicit clear. Disconnect clears the
client retention. Late replies keep their existing opening guards.

Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5 against BDS 1.26.60.6/protocol 2225 retains the
latest query and explicit clear after pointer close and reopening. Its first
Escape while editing removes search focus; the next Escape closes the
inventory. Reproduce that focus order in the actual editor. An installed
Java control with search handling delayed by four seconds loses visible
text before this change. Controller boundary tests cover queued text and
clearing across close/reopen, late replies, blocked item actions while a
new search is pending, and connection replacement.

Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5/BDS 1.26.60.6 protocol-2225 controls delay
ContainerOpen by four seconds. Native retains the world and gameplay
movement until acknowledgment. Repeated E resends the interaction with
the same pending intent. Escape and chat cancel it; a late acknowledgment
closes the abandoned native container without replacing those screens.

Negotiate acknowledgment-bound player inventory requests separately from
the legacy bridge. Intercept actual GUI installation and retain the desired
screen behind the world until its own ready token arrives. Repeated input
retains its token, another screen retires it, and late or old-connection
readiness cannot display it. Keep read-only snapshots against that pending
owner without displaying them or enabling item actions before acknowledgment.

Apply the gate independently of the authored inventory visual setting.
Older translators retain their existing immediate-screen behavior because
they do not advertise the acknowledgment-bound request channel. Workbench
screens already enter through the server open packet and need no extra gate.

Retain the admitted opening through initialization redirects, including
Java InventoryScreen redirecting to Creative before GUI installation
returns. The installed control exposed an unnecessary close/reopen cycle;
a controller regression preserves its token and checks for one request.

Check the current player menu before accepting readiness. Replacement
player menus retire the pending token instead of displaying a screen
captured from the old player. A controller regression covers that boundary.

Route authored container clips through the shared physical-pixel scissor
path. Preserve fractional layout bounds and native parent intersections
instead of rounding them to Java GUI units before extraction. Container
items, labels, portraits and nested inputs retain their existing ownership
and share the same bounded render-state clipping lifecycle.

Use inherited scissors for admitted container labels, matching the shared
scene renderer. The shared renderer limits complete text rows and fits the
last-row marker. Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5 MinecraftTen glyphs can extend
past a label's own height while retaining the parent clip.

Preserve the player search editor, selection and active text admission
through real Screen rebuilds. Register the retained widget after each
rebuild and leave an inactive editor inactive on opening or resizing.
Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 local-world controls retain active
Creative and Survival input through viewport and GUI-scale changes.

Keep text-input ownership separate from editor highlighting. Native typing
resumes after Escape or a blank-area click, but selecting another recipe
or slot retires that admission. Respect authored handle_select and
handle_deselect flags, and release SDL input when the editor disappears
or its screen exits. Full Screen lifecycle regressions retain Ctrl+A
selection and exercise blur, re-entry, another control and closing.

Render opening-scoped crafting previews independently of recipe cells and
actual menu contents. Use the authored unavailable-item background, counts
and hover tooltips only over empty slots. Real items and active drag
previews retain precedence. Preserve previews through search and retire
them when the player inventory opening changes.

Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5 controls show an unavailable oak-planks recipe
with a log ghost and four-plank output, no inventory request, persistence
through search and blank focus, and clearing after reopening. Focused
presentation tests cover ownership, real-slot precedence and lifetime.
These tests do not establish complete native GPU or variant parity.

Resolve count and durability controls from the eligible crafting ghost
when no item draw has populated the per-frame preview. Authored painter
order must not decide the display stack. Share the same empty-slot and
active-drag admission with item rendering, while retaining real stacks
and vanilla draw overrides. A renderer-boundary test draws count first,
then verifies real-item precedence and suppression during drag selection.

Decode physical bundle preview tables directly from retained typed item
metadata. Bind the table to its native identity and slot amounts before
converting complete Java component patches through the Minecraft item
codec. Keep holes and native primary names, and own copies of decoded
stacks so caller mutations cannot change later scene bindings. Reject
incomplete, malformed or count-changing conversions instead of substituting
Java bundle contents. Forward explicit native tooltip views through storage,
cooking, player and workbench presentation boundaries; existing callers
retain inactive views until source lifetime and renderer admission are wired.
Tests exercise the actual Minecraft item codec and authored scene resolution.
The screen's storage-tooltip guard remains in place; this does not establish
native input, placement, physical-slot actions or installed pixel parity.

Pass the desktop native-UI pointer anchor to shared type-3 placement and
retain the bundle renderer inside-parent override. Recompute an active
placement when the pointer changes without invalidating unrelated static
layouts. The existing bundle admission guards remain until native source
lifetimes, physical-slot item rendering and input actions are connected.
Original Bedrock 1.26.60.5 renderer 144c81946 forces bit8; its placement
semantics are covered by the shared layout patch.

Retain primary, pending, secondary, hovered and bundle source contexts
independently of recipe hover and Java bundle ordinals. Resolve tooltip
frames against the current exported menu source and cache only unchanged
item snapshots. Ordinary inactive hover does not invalidate bundle layout.
